    {
      "date": "-4600000000",
      "headline": "Formation of the Sun",
      "text": "The Sun formed at the center of the collapsing molecular cloud. It is a G-type main-sequence star, which means it is in a stable phase of hydrogen fusion in its core, releasing energy that supports life on Earth."
    },
    {
      "date": "-4500000000",
      "headline": "Formation of the Earth and Other Planets",
      "text": "The planets, including Earth, formed from the protoplanetary disc surrounding the young Sun. Over time, particles collided and stuck together, forming planetesimals and eventually the planets."
    },
    {
      "date": "-4500000000",
      "headline": "Formation of the Moon",
      "text": "The Moon is believed to have formed shortly after Earth, around 4.5 billion years ago, likely as a result of a giant impact between Earth and a Mars-sized body."
    },
    {
      "date": "-3800000000",
      "headline": "Late Heavy Bombardment",
      "text": "Around 3.8 billion years ago, the Solar System experienced a period of intense comet and asteroid bombardment, which significantly affected the surfaces of the terrestrial planets and moons."
    },
    {
      "date": "-3500000000",
      "headline": "Emergence of Life on Earth",
      "text": "Life is believed to have emerged on Earth around 3.5 billion years ago, during the early Archean era, as evidenced by fossilized microorganisms found in ancient rocks."
    },
    {
      "date": "-1000000000",
      "headline": "Formation of the Kuiper Belt",
      "text": "The Kuiper Belt, a region of the Solar System beyond the orbit of Neptune, is believed to have formed around 1 billion years ago, containing many small icy bodies and dwarf planets like Pluto."
    },
    {
      "date": "1543",
      "headline": "Copernican Revolution",
      "text": "Nicolaus Copernicus published 'De revolutionibus orbium coelestium', proposing a heliocentric model of the Solar System, which placed the Sun at the center rather than the Earth."
    },
    {
      "date": "1610",
      "headline": "Galileo's Observations",
      "text": "Galileo Galilei used a telescope to observe celestial bodies, discovering the four largest moons of Jupiter and providing evidence for the heliocentric model of the Solar System."
    },
    {
      "date": "1687",
      "headline": "Newton's Principia",
      "text": "Isaac Newton published 'Philosophiæ Naturalis Principia Mathematica', which formulated the laws of motion and universal gravitation, explaining the orbits of planets and moons in the Solar System."
    },
    {
