      "text": "The Ibadi Imamate was established in Oman, marking the beginning of a unique Islamic tradition that continues to influence Omani culture and politics."
    },
    {
      "date": "1507",
      "headline": "Portuguese Occupation",
      "text": "The Portuguese captured Muscat and other coastal areas of Oman, establishing a foothold in the region to control trade routes."
    },
    {
      "date": "1650",
      "headline": "Expulsion of the Portuguese",
      "text": "The Omanis, under the leadership of Imam Sultan bin Saif, expelled the Portuguese from Muscat, ending their control over the region."
    },
    {
      "date": "1749",
      "headline": "Al Said Dynasty",
      "text": "The Al Said dynasty was established by Ahmad bin Said, who became the ruler of Oman after expelling the Persian invaders."
    },
    {
      "date": "1832",
      "headline": "Zanzibar Sultanate",
      "text": "Sultan Said bin Sultan moved the capital of his empire to Zanzibar, establishing a powerful sultanate that controlled trade in the Indian Ocean."
    },
    {
      "date": "1970-07-23",
      "headline": "Sultan Qaboos's Reign Begins",
      "text": "Sultan Qaboos bin Said al Said overthrew his father in a bloodless coup, initiating a period of modernization and development in Oman."
    },
    {
      "date": "1996",
      "headline": "Basic Statute of the State",
      "text": "Sultan Qaboos issued the Basic Statute of the State, which serves as the constitution of Oman, outlining the structure of the government and the rights of citizens."
    },
    {
      "date": "2011",
      "headline": "Arab Spring Protests",
      "text": "Oman experienced protests as part of the Arab Spring, leading to political reforms and increased government spending on job creation and social benefits."
    },
    {
      "date": "2020-01-11",
      "headline": "Sultan Haitham bin Tariq",
      "text": "Sultan Haitham bin Tariq al Said became the Sultan of Oman following the death of Sultan Qaboos, continuing the policies of modernization and development."
