      "headline": "Year-Round DST in the U.S.",
      "text": "During World War II, President Franklin D. Roosevelt instituted year-round daylight saving time, known as 'War Time,' from February 9, 1942, to September 30, 1945."
    },
    {
      "date": "1966",
      "headline": "Uniform Time Act",
      "text": "The United States passed the Uniform Time Act, which standardized the start and end dates for daylight saving time across the country, although states could opt out."
    },
    {
      "date": "1973",
      "headline": "Oil Crisis and DST Extension",
      "text": "In response to the 1973 oil crisis, the United States Congress extended daylight saving time in an effort to save energy."
    },
    {
      "date": "1986",
      "headline": "DST Start Date Change",
      "text": "The United States Congress changed the start date of daylight saving time from the last Sunday in April to the first Sunday in April."
    },
    {
      "date": "2007",
      "headline": "Energy Policy Act of 2005",
      "text": "The Energy Policy Act of 2005 extended daylight saving time in the United States, starting it on the second Sunday in March and ending on the first Sunday in November."
