      "text": "The Druze faith was founded in the early 11th century during the reign of the sixth Fatimid caliph, al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah. The religion emerged as a distinct faith in 1017, when the call to the new faith was publicly proclaimed."
    },
    {
      "date": "1021",
      "headline": "Disappearance of al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah",
      "text": "The disappearance of al-Hakim bi-Amr Allah, a central figure in the Druze faith, occurred in 1021. His disappearance is considered a significant event in the history of the Druze as he is regarded as a divine figure by the adherents."
    },
    {
      "date": "1043",
      "headline": "End of the Druze Proselytization",
      "text": "In 1043, the Druze faith closed its doors to new converts, marking the end of its proselytization phase. This decision was made by the Druze leadership to preserve the purity and secrecy of their religious teachings."
    },
    {
      "date": "1860",
      "headline": "Mount Lebanon Civil War",
      "text": "The Druze played a significant role in the Mount Lebanon civil war of 1860, which was a conflict between the Druze and the Maronite Christians. The war resulted in significant loss of life and changes in the political landscape of the region."
    },
    {
      "date": "1921",
      "headline": "Establishment of Jabal al-Druze State",
      "text": "In 1921, the French Mandate authorities established the State of Jabal al-Druze in southern Syria, providing the Druze with a degree of autonomy. This state existed until 1936 when it was reintegrated into Syria."
    },
    {
      "date": "1948",
      "headline": "Druze in the Arab-Israeli Conflict",
      "text": "During the 1948 Arab-Israeli War, many Druze in Israel chose to side with the newly established state of Israel. This decision has had lasting impacts on the Druze community in Israel and their relationship with the state."
    },
    {
      "date": "1982",
      "headline": "Druze in the Lebanese Civil War",
      "text": "The Druze community, led by Walid Jumblatt, played a significant role in the Lebanese Civil War, particularly in the Chouf Mountains. The Druze militia was a key player in the conflict, which lasted from 1975 to 1990."
