      "headline": "Bhopal Disaster Begins",
      "text": "On the night of December 2, 1984, a chemical accident occurred at the Union Carbide India Limited (UCIL) pesticide plant in Bhopal, Madhya Pradesh, India. A highly toxic gas, methyl isocyanate (MIC), was released into the atmosphere."
    },
    {
      "date": "1984-12-03",
      "headline": "Immediate Impact of the Gas Leak",
      "text": "By the morning of December 3, 1984, over 500,000 people in the surrounding areas were exposed to the toxic gas. The official number of immediate deaths was reported as 2,259."
    },
    {
      "date": "1984-12",
      "headline": "Aftermath and Initial Response",
      "text": "In the weeks following the disaster, estimates suggested that around 8,000 people died due to the gas exposure. The incident was recognized as the world's worst industrial disaster."
    },
    {
      "date": "2006",
      "headline": "Government Affidavit on Injuries",
      "text": "A government affidavit stated that the gas leak caused 558,125 injuries, including 38,478 temporary partial injuries and approximately 3,900 severely and permanently disabling injuries."
    },
    {
      "date": "2008",
      "headline": "Compensation to Victims",
      "text": "The Government of Madhya Pradesh paid compensation to the family members of 3,787 victims killed in the gas release and to 574,366 injured victims."
    }
  ]
}

